JWasm: -Zj gives a forward-referenced constant operand a memory size

In pass one, MASM 5 doesn't know what a forward reference is, and sizes
a second operand that has one, after a general register, as memory: a
16-bit immediate. When the value turns out to be a small constant, the
instruction is still that long in pass two (a NOP pads it). JWasm sized
it as a constant, sign-extended to 8 bits.

The same holds for an EQU whose expression has a forward reference in
pass one: MASM 5 leaves it undefined until pass two, so an instruction
that uses it has a forward reference. JWasm makes such an EQU a text
macro; it is now marked, and an operand that expands it counts as a
forward reference.

Needed for MODE's RESCODE.ASM, SUB AX,adjustment_in_paragraphs
(2D 0A 00 90 in MASM 5's object).

